# Next.js route handlers

## Return types

Every handler needs an explicit return type (`NextResponse` or `Response`).
Never return `null` or implicit `undefined` on error paths. Use explicit
`NextResponse.json(...)` (or equivalent).

## Thin backend

Validate input, call a query/RPC/service, shape the response. Do not implement
heavy domain math in the handler; read pre-aggregated views when they exist.

## External vendors

Do not call paid/external vendor HTTP APIs from route handlers when the project
already ingests that data into the DB. Read local tables. Enqueue ingest on
cache miss via the project's documented RPC — do not duplicate eligibility
logic across routes.

## Secrets

Server-only secrets never leak to the client. Admin/service-role clients stay
in server contexts.
